53
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:40.380
Finally split long right sides

54
00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:42.400
into binary ones and isolate

55
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.100
terminals mixed with variables. Introduce

56
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:46.600
A one becoming S A,

57
00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:49.460
so A S A can

58
00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:50.960
be replaced by A A

59
00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:54.000
one. Introduce U becoming a,

60
00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:55.620
so a B becomes U

61
00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:58.860
B, keeping already legal single-terminal

62
00:01:58.860 --> 00:01:59.800
rules unchanged.

63
00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:03.140
Check the resulting rules individually.

64
00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:05.140
S zero and S can

65
00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:07.180
become A A one, U

66
00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:09.720
B, a, S A or

67
00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.060
A S; A has those

68
00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:13.200
options plus b, and B

69
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.740
becomes b. Every right side

70
00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:17.280
is now either exactly two

71
00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.580
variables or exactly one terminal,

72
00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:21.320
and S zero is never

73
00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:22.240
on a right side.

74
00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:24.380
For a word of length

75
00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.120
n at least one, a

76
00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:28.300
binary Chomsky-form parse tree has

77
00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:31.000
n terminal-producing leaves. It requires

78
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.800
n minus one binary branch

79
00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.520
expansions to grow from one

80
00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:36.300
variable to n variable leaves,

81
00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.180
and then n terminal productions.

82
00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.200
The total is exactly two

83
00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.620
n minus one production applications,

84
00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:44.300
independent of their order.

85
00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.600
For instance, deriving a b

86
00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:47.860
from S zero through U

87
00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:50.340
B takes three applications: the

88
00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:51.760
binary production and the two

89
00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:54.600
terminal productions. This matches twice

90
00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:56.560
two minus one. A finite

91
00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:59.400
derivation-length bound makes exhaustive membership

92
00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.320
search finite, but does not

93
00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.220
by itself make that exhaustive

94
00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:03.980
search polynomial.

95
00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:05.880
Things to take note of.

96
00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:08.540
Preserve nullable alternatives and unit

97
00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:11.340
reachability before binarizing, and keep

98
00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:12.840
the fresh start off right-hand

99
00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:15.240
sides. Chomsky normal form is

100
00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:16.960
different from conjunctive normal form

101
00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:19.280
in SAT; the appendix’s excluded

102
00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:20.880
theorem proof does not exclude

103
00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.820
performing this conversion or using

104
00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:24.200
the two n minus one

105
00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:24.780
consequence.
